 built around co-op  allowing friends to drop  in and out with repeatable missions now  let's zoom out a little and take a look  at ubisoft's most successful  aaa franchises assassin's creed odyssey  far cry 5 and watch dogs 2  are all open world games with skill  trees and some form of character  customization  sure assassin's creed and watch dogs  don't let you create your character but  you can still change their looks with  armor and clothes and of course you can  play both watch dogs 2 and far cry 2  cooperatively from start to finish  this familiarity between titles was  largely due to ubisoft's editorial team  which gave a handful of people a lot of  influence over ubisoft's games  so then the question is how do you do  all that in a splinter cell game  you can't get rid of sam because sam is  at the center of the series so that  rules out player creation  you could let players customize sam's  uniform but you can only go so far with  that  sam is a professional with years of  experience under his belt  i can't imagine he'd wanted to play with  anything other than a black or camo  jumpsuit you also can't really give sam  an unlockable skill tree for the same  reason  sam is a professional and he shouldn't  be unlocking faster takedowns or  whatever  multiple decades into his military  career however you could get around this  with unlockable gear and weapons  co-op on the other hand is an  interesting one because chaos theory  conviction  and blacklist had excellent co-op  missions but those games were designed  first and foremost the single player in  mind apart from blacklist  co-op felt like its own separate entity  in fact most of what i just proposed was  part of splinter cell blacklist  there were light customization options  as you complete admissions you earn cash  that let you purchase new tech and  weapons and the co-op was  naturally interwoven with the single  player campaign the issue as you know by  now was that blacklist  underperformed and that michael ironside  didn't voice sam for [ __ ] sake who is  andre cobin  andre cobin pretended to kill my  daughter to give someone leverage over  me  so now let's go back to what eve said in  the interview you have to come up with  something that will be different enough  from what you did before  by all accounts i'd say blacklist did  this but when you look at the direction  ubisoft was heading at the time  splinter cell was a square peg being  jammed into a round hole  so if ubisoft's attempt to modernize  splinter cell failed then what does that  mean for the future of the series  well it means a lot of waiting like i  said earlier ubisoft  knows the value of the splinter cell ip  i find it really hard to believe that we  won't see another splinter cell game but  when you consider blacklist and  ubisoft's current portfolio it's hard to  see how a new splinter cell game could  fit in  however there's one thing i haven't  touched on yet we live in a  post metal gear solid world throughout  splinter cell's entire history it had  been competing with one of the most  well-known video game franchises of all  time  metal gear solid without the promise of  a new metal gear game on the horizon  there is a growing stealth action game  void in the market and of course  ubisoft knows this too yeah a few i've  heard they don't make them like me  anymore  there was this other guy though army  infiltration he wore  a bandana or something i heard he  finally retired  really yep it's only me  better yet metal gear solid 5 the  phantom pain proved that not only can  you make an  open world stealth game but you can make  a damn fine one  while i'd still prefer a more linear  experience i think there's a lot of  potential for an open world splinter  cell game if ubisoft builds off metal  gear solid 5's excellent foundation  maybe instead of two big maps like an  mgs5  there can be multiple smaller semi-open  world maps with a wide array of  objectives and a day night cycle to give  you and sam some tactical options  they could even have a similar r d  mechanic that lets you invest in  research new third echelon tech for sam  to use on the field the other more  important thing to consider is that  metal gear solid 5 was a big seller  selling over 6 million copies before the  end of 2015.  the biggest hurdle here would be finding  a way to tell a meaningful splinter cell  story with sam fisher at the center of  it  at this point i think a reboot would  make the most sense rhett cunning all  the events of previous games cause it  did get kinda messy near the end there  but you have to imagine that the same  day konami and kojima parted ways  ubisoft had a meeting about sam fisher  and the future of the splinter cell  series  but once again it's really hard to  imagine what a splinter cell game would  be like today with ubisoft's modern game  design principles in mind  and if i'm being honest i have a really  hard time picturing a new splinter cell  game staying true to its history under  these design philosophies  the last thing i want is an open world  live service splinter cell game with  micro transactions and a heavy emphasis  on multiplayer i know this is a rather  pessimistic take but if you look at  ubisoft's current lineup it's hard to  imagine that a new splinter cell  wouldn't follow suit  but there is a glimmer of hope after  breakpoint's disappointing launch eve  mentioned that one of the major  issues it faced was that it didn't stand  out in the industry amidst its own games  remember that ubisoft editorial team i  was talking about earlier while ubisoft  plans to rethink how that team  operates this restructuring was possibly  why so many of its games including  watchdog's legion got pushed  i can't think of anything that would  stand out more among ubisoft's tom  clancy portfolio than a somewhat linear  single player focused splinter cell game  especially with the new consoles on the  way imagine a next-gen splinter cell  game l